---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro"
import Link from "../../components/Link.astro"
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import plain_api from "../../img/web/plain-api.png"
---
<Slide t1="Weather API" t2="Fetch and Display in React">
    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <li class="fragment">No back-end Flask (for the moment):
            React fetching directly from network API (OpenWeatherMap 2.x)
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">Fetch JSON data and display in-page as text</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ plain_api }
            alt="Plain Weather API"
            height="200"
            width="300" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Starter Code">
    <div class="text-sm-2">
        <CodeBlock lang="jsx" height="500px" lines="*|2-3|12,20,27">
            { `
function App() { ...
    const [weatherData, setWeatherData] = useState("")
    const [location, setLocation] = useState("")

    function createURL(loc) { ... }

    async function getWeatherData(loc) {
        const url = createURL(loc)
        let result = {}

        try {
            const response = await fetch(url)

            console.log("Received response")

            if (!response.ok) {
                throw new Error(\`Failed fetching \${url}\`)
            }
            else {
                result = await response.json() // parses JSON response into native JavaScript objects
            }
        } catch (error) {
            result.error = error
            console.error("There has been a problem with your fetch operation:", error)
        }

        setWeatherData(result)
    }
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="React Fragment">
    <div class="text-sm-2">
        <CodeBlock lang="jsx" height="450px">
            { `
<div className="main">
    {LOCATIONS.map(
        (loc, i) => (
            <button key={i} onClick={ () => {
                            setLocation(loc)
                            getWeatherData(loc)
                        }
                    }
                    >{loc}</button>
        )
    ) }

    <button onClick={ () => {setLocation(""); setWeatherData("")} }>
        CLEAR
    </button>

    <div>Location: { location || "none" }</div>
    <textarea
        readonly
        rows="20"
        cols="40"
        value={ JSON.stringify(weatherData,null,2) } />
</div>
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Exercises">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">
            Warm up: add some more locations across the globe (with a wide
                spread of climates). Format: <Link href="https://openweathermap.org/find" />
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            Group the locations by country (Q: when might it be useful to have multiple
                locations per country?)
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            How can this bare-bones interface be improved?
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            What useful data can be pulled out of the JSON payload?
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            How can it be visualised?
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Ideas">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">
            <code>weather</code> has a <code>main</code> title,
            a <code>description</code> and an <code>icon</code>
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            <code>main</code> has a temperature with minimum and
            maximum values - and also a "feels like" figure
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            <code>wind</code> has a speed and direction
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            <code>clouds</code> has (this is a guess...) an amount (percentage?) of cover
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            Quite a bit harder: what about some kind of map (or at least latitude/longitude) display?
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Worked Example (Daylight Hours)">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Some simple data plotting: HTML only</li>
        <li class="fragment">Useful library: install Math.js via <code>npm</code>
            (how are we using this in the example?)</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Stretch Goal">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Sunrise/sunset as a continuum (coloured bar) rather than HTML text</li>
        <li class="fragment">Is it possible to mark the current time against this?</li>
        <ul class="fragment">
            <li><span class="text-red-500">Warning</span>: think about the timezones</li>
        </ul>
        <li class="fragment">What about something similar for temperature range?
            ("Feels like" might be out of range...)
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>
